  "canonical": {
    "definition": "Mining operations present EXTREME contamination exposure — silica and hardrock particulate\nconcentrations 50× above ISO 5011 test standards — requiring multi-system filtration\nprotection across air intake, hydraulic, lube oil, and fuel domains for 24/7 asset\nreliability.",
    "systems": "Air Intake, Hydraulic, Lubrication, Fuel Cleanliness; primary equipment: haul trucks,\ndrill rigs, loaders, crushers operating in open-cut and underground environments",
    "failure_impact": "Uncontrolled particle ingestion → engine overhaul interval reduced from 25,000 to\n3,000 hours → unplanned downtime at ~$180,000/hour per machine → cascading supply\nchain disruption | Hydraulic contamination → proportional valve stiction → haul truck\nhydraulic failure → production stoppage",
    "related_standards": "ISO 5011: Air filter efficiency and restriction test | ISO 16889: Hydraulic cleanliness\nmulti-pass test | SAE J1539: Air intake contamination classification for diesel engines",
    "related_technologies": "MACROCORE: Engine air filtration for primary and secondary intake protection |\nNANOFORCE: Sub-micron hydraulic contamination control (ISO 4406 16/14/11) |\nSYNTRAX: Full-flow lube oil protection (extended drain intervals) |\nTURBOCORE: Fuel water separation (condensation and bulk fuel contamination control)",
    "industrial_role": "Mining is the highest-consequence filtration environment — equipment downtime costs\nexceed $180,000/hour and component replacement costs reach $150,000+ per engine\noverhaul. Contamination control is a supply-chain continuity decision, not a\nmaintenance cost decision."
  },
